---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO_URL } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base title="About" current="about" description="Why the Connecticut Open Access Observatory exists, who made it, and the principles it follows.">
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>About this site</h1>
    <p class="deck">
      A free, public look at how much research from Connecticut’s colleges and universities anyone can read without a
      subscription.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why">Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Libraries pay for journal subscriptions, support open access publishing, and run repositories where authors can share
      their work. Decisions about all three need evidence: how much research is already free, which routes it takes, where
      the paywalled articles are, and what authors may be paying to publish. This site brings those figures together for
      17 Connecticut institutions, from open data, with its methods and limits in the open.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is meant for librarians, scholarly communication staff, research offices, and anyone curious about open access
      in the state.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="principles">
    <h2 id="principles">Principles</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Institutions, not people.</strong> The site reports totals for institutions. It has no pages about individual researchers.</li>
      <li><strong>Describe, don’t rank.</strong> Institutions differ in size, subjects and funding. The figures are context for decisions, not a league table.</li>
      <li><strong>Show the working.</strong> Every number comes from open data and a published script. Estimates and proxies are labeled as such, and the <a href="/methods/">Methods page</a> says where the numbers fall short.</li>
      <li><strong>No tracking.</strong> No cookies, analytics or accounts, and nothing is loaded from other servers.</li>
      <li><strong>Open data.</strong> All the tables can be <a href="/downloads/">downloaded</a> and reused freely.</li>
      <li><strong>Accessible.</strong> Every chart has a written summary and a data table, and the site is built to meet WCAG 2.2 AA.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="who">
    <h2 id="who">Who made it</h2>
    <p>
      The site was built by Chris Misciagna, a librarian with an MLIS from the University of South Carolina, as an
      independent project. It is not affiliated with any of the
      institutions it covers, or with OpenAlex.
    </p>
    <p>
      Corrections and questions are welcome through the project’s <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>GitHub issues page</a>.
      If you work at one of these institutions and see something that looks wrong, such as missing repository copies or
      misassigned articles, that is especially useful to hear.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
